WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.250
What’s up?

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.250
This is Jack Peterson.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:10.070
I recently finished my first feature film
which is called SAWGRASS and I’m making

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:13.240
this video at the request of Film Courage.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.300
So this is just going to be 5 tips for your
first micro budget feature film.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.980
Tip #1 - Put In The Work:

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.300
To tip number one is basically put in the
work.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.939
Don’t be lazy and basically it takes a long
time to finish these things.

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:33.470
From the time I came up for the idea of SAWGRASS
to the time I finished it was basically three

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:34.470
years.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:40.390
So it takes a significant amount of time and
energy and focus to actually finish a full-length

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.760
film.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:49.190
SAWGRASS was 89-minutes long and like I said
it took 3 years from inception to completion

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.469
so it’s not easy.

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:55.600
Even if you make something relatively short
like I did it’s still going to take a long

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.930
time.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.739
I feel like my second feature film might be
a little bit of a smoother process now that

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:06.360
I have some experience and now that I know
what it takes to complete something but still

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.100
it’s a really arduous process.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.600
You can’t be lazy and you have to put in
the work even if you don’t feel like it.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:20.170
Even if you are having a bad day, it’s just
required if you actually want to finish something.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:26.810
There are a lot of people that start a film
or a novel or an album (whatever the case

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:31.700
may be) and it never gets done pretty much
because they don’t have the discipline to

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.020
see it through.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:38.400
So that’s pretty much the most important
thing in this section is just to put in the

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:46.659
work and actually try and finish the film
instead of starting and stopping and starting

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:51.950
again a year later because if you are not
focused it’s never going to get done.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:56.049
Tip #2 - Scale Back If Necessary:

00:01:56.049 --> 00:02:03.540
When I initially finished the script for SAWGRASS
it was like 93-pages long and it was super-ambitious

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.040
and it had all kinds of live action scenes
that I was going to film and all of these

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:13.590
extensive special effects sequences that I
had no idea how I was going to accomplish.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:21.200
I had this naive idea that I’d be able to
raise financing for this crazy thing without

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.080
any prior experience.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.270
To be honest I didn’t really pursue financing
too much because I quickly realized it was

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.569
not going to work.

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:33.459
So I decided I would scale back and do something
small.

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:37.000
So I pretty much did a small micro-budget
version of the same script.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.160
I scaled back a lot of things and I changed
a lot of the elements around and made it more…well

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.780
I put more documentary elements in it and
I did a lot of animation rather than live

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:56.489
action special effects so I simplified it
and kept the core of what it was.

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:00.700
I scaled it back so I could actually do it
on the budget that I had.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.230
And as far as what the budget actually was
it was so small that I didn’t actually even

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.230
count it.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.850
I just spent money as I needed to.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.450
So it might have been a few thousand dollars.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.760
But regardless it was probably smaller than
what it would have been if I had tried to

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.130
accomplish the original script so just tried
to scale back if required.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.879
If you can get a lot of money your first go-around,
that’s cool.

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:31.630
Most people won’t so just try and do something
that you can feasibly finish with the budget

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.520
that you are working with.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.090
Tip #3: Don’t Be Afraid To Reach Out:

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:41.870
Pretty much what this means is if there is
somebody you want to work with or if you have

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:48.700
a question for somebody that you are really
inspired by don’t be afraid to reach out

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:55.030
to them in some way whether that is on Twitter
or email or even a phone call which is basically

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.569
what I did.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:01.700
So when I first finished the script for this
thing I was trying to get anybody I could

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:07.270
to just read it, that I admired who could
give me some actual feedback.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:12.090
The first person I called was actually Bob
Odenkirk (from BREAKING BAD and BETTER CALL

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:13.090
SAUL).

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.670
And I just cold called him out of the blue.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.239
And at first he was like “Who the heck are
you?”

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:26.170
basically and I explained who I was, what
I was doing and the film I was making.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:31.000
He politely told me that he didn’t have
time to read scripts but he talked to me for

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.060
20 minutes and gave me tons of useful career
advice and tons of information on how to finish

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:42.100
a film and what I should do going forward
and he gave me a lot of motivation that was

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.240
really valuable in actually finishing the
film.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:50.110
So does Bob Odenkirk remember me a year later
or whatever it is?

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:51.110
Probably not.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.270
But the fact that I spoke to him and got all
of that information from him is part of the

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.860
reason that the film is actually completed
now.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.620
Another guy I cold called at one point was
a guy named Ken Levine who is the creator

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.080
of BIOSHOCK and he’s a really good writer.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.430
So I talked to him and he gave me similarly
tons of good advice and inspiration.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:17.060
And a lot of motivation to actually finish
something.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:23.900
He gave me feedback on a short film I made
which was super valuable and really amazing

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.620
to get something like that from someone I
admire.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:31.210
So never be afraid to reach out to someone.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:35.259
The worst they will do is tell you that they
can’t help you.

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:37.699
They are not going to be mean to you.

00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:44.550
I’ve never once had someone that I cold
called insult me or tell me off because I

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.020
called them.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.270
Most people just want to help so always feel
free to reach out to anyone that you want

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:55.509
to work with or want advice from (whatever).

00:05:55.509 --> 00:06:02.960
That is how I got Erick Paddock the brother
of the Vegas to help with my movie and I was

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.410
able to interview him for the movie and it
was great and it was all just because I reached

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.160
out to him.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:15.990
So that’s a really valuable thing to learn
is that there is no one that you can’t cold

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.699
call or email if you really want to.

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:23.569
Tip #4 - Don’t Worry About Film Festivals
and Distribution:

00:06:23.569 --> 00:06:29.210
I think a lot of people probably disagree
with this but I’ve seen a million people

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:36.060
make a micro-budget feature films (especially
their first ones) and they submit to a ton

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:42.759
of festivals, they basically don’t get into
any and then they try to get a distribution

00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:44.710
deal and sometimes they do.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:49.889
Like I was talking to a girl recently who
found some obscure distributor that put her

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:57.069
movie on VOD basically and she has made zero
money back from that and probably no one has

00:06:57.069 --> 00:06:59.880
seen the film to be honest with you.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:07.350
I really doubt that anyone is going to pay
$5.00 to see some movie by a no-named filmmaker

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:15.210
with no-name actors in it so I would highly
recommend that you put your work out for free

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:22.420
early on and then later on down the road you
can start charging for it or move on to bigger

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:23.420
things.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:29.240
But when you’re starting out you have to
put out free samples and if people like it

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:34.690
they will help you fund a bigger project next
time.

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:39.100
But yes, don’t worry too much about film
festivals and distribution deals and all of

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:44.819
that stuff because in the end what really
matters at first is developing yourself as

00:07:44.819 --> 00:07:47.110
a filmmaker.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.680
Tip #5 - Consider It A Long-Term Investment

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:55.280
This basically ties into my point about not
worrying about film festivals and distribution

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.639
because there are some filmmakers who take
10 or 15 years before ever making any money

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:01.639
off of their film.

00:08:01.639 --> 00:08:05.349
So that’s a common story and it could happen
to you.

00:08:05.349 --> 00:08:06.770
So you can’t give up.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.920
You just have to keep making films.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.389
The truth is most filmmakers never even go
on to make one feature film and most of the

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:19.090
filmmakers who make one feature film, never
make a second feature film.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:22.910
So it’s just about getting past that and
continuing to make films despite the lack

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:25.680
of visible progress.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.440
It’s not something that happens overnight.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.300
You have to work it up over time.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.479
My second feature film is a little bit more
ambitious.

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:37.349
I’m going to try and raise more money this
time.

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:41.280
And then my third film after that will probably
be even more ambitious.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.529
So it’s just something that takes a long
time to achieve basically.

00:08:46.529 --> 00:08:49.760
So you have to look at it long term.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.089
Those are my five tips so hopefully somebody
finds this video useful.

00:08:54.089 --> 00:08:55.300
Thanks again to Film Courage.

